    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9f61e9f0-74bc-578a-849d-22279a5eebc7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $t^2-3t+2$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$t^2-3t+2=(t-$ {{field-1}} $)^2-$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^2-3t+2&=\\underbrace{t^2-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{9}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{9}{4}+2 \\\\\n&=\\left(t-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{9}{4}+2 \\\\\n&=\\left(t-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{1}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-3t$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
